1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:06,640
Welcome to, uh, today's deep dive. So today, we are unpacking the published identity building block within the reference architecture.

2
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:13,420
And, I mean, if you run a ministry program, you might assume your national identity authority just offers everything you need out of the box, right?

3
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,940
Right. Exactly. But, um,

4
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:22,780
assuming instead of checking can lead to fragmented, duplicated systems that are incredibly costly to change.

5
00:00:23,260 --> 00:00:24,800
So our mission today is to break down

6
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:33,200
exactly what this foundational building block actually offers, how it protects data, and, well, why reality often differs from the specification.

7
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:38,040
Yeah, because, you know, when you build a new house, you expect the municipal plumbing to just work.

8
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:46,180
You turn the tap, water flows. You definitely don't expect to have to build a custom water tower for the bathroom and then, like, a, a completely separate one for the kitchen.

9
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:46,920
No, of course not.

10
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:52,700
But in government IT, it is surprisingly easy to accidentally build those custom water towers.

11
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:53,100
Yeah.

12
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:54,680
So to start us off,

13
00:00:55,480 --> 00:01:01,620
what exactly does this building block cover, and, uh, why doesn't it issue things like student or healthcare IDs?

14
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:08,040
Well, the gap between what is written in the specification and what a national authority actually runs today

15
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:10,600
is, um, it's pretty significant.

16
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:18,200
If we look at the architecture, there is a very strict boundary drawn between foundational identity and functional identity.

17
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:20,040
Okay, so what's the difference there?

18
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:25,140
So foundational identity is simply the proof of who a person broadly is.

19
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,320
It serves a wide range of public and private services.

20
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:33,700
Functional identity, on the other hand, is proof used for one specific purpose or sector.

21
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:42,620
Oh, I see. So if I am running an education program, I might expect this central identity block to issue a functional identity, like a,

22
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:44,000
a learner number or something.

23
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:52,000
Exactly. But based on that boundary, the block only handles foundational proof, so it offers specific services like enrollment,

24
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:58,760
identity verification, queries, and credential management to administrators, partners, users, and subscribers.

25
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:02,320
Right. But it issues absolutely no learner identity.

26
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:07,860
The specification explicitly names sectors like education as functional domains outside its scope.

27
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:11,880
Its only job is to verify the person behind the screen.

28
00:02:12,260 --> 00:02:21,520
Wait, hold on. If a registered partner, like the education ministry, uses this to verify someone, aren't they just broadcasting the citizen's national ID number everywhere?

29
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:24,240
I mean, that seems like a massive privacy vulnerability.

30
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:30,540
It absolutely would be, and that is exactly why the core security mechanism is designed to prevent that.

31
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:35,320
The unique national identity number stays entirely secret inside the building block.

32
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,560
Oh, really? So it's essentially like a bouncer at a club.

33
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:45,620
The bouncer checks my driver's license to verify who I am, but they don't hand my home address or my license number over to the bartender.

34
00:02:46,200 --> 00:02:48,560
Right. Yeah. That is a perfect way to look at it.

35
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,000
They just give the bartender a thumbs up or, like, a generic wristband.

36
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:54,840
So how does that translate technically here?

37
00:02:55,540 --> 00:03:00,560
Well, the published interfaces rely on OpenID Connect, which is a common standard for signing in.

38
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:07,280
When a citizen interacts with your ministry service, their browser is taken directly to the identity block's own screens.

39
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:10,060
Okay, so the citizen is directly in the loop there.

40
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:15,700
Yes, exactly. The person signs in there and actively approves what data may be shared.

41
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:19,600
In return, the block releases only that approved data.

42
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:23,400
And instead of the national ID number, the ministry gets the wristband.

43
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:26,480
Right. We call that wristband a token.

44
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:31,440
Each relying service receives a completely different service-specific token identifier.

45
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:33,560
Oh, wow, so they don't even get the same token.

46
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:34,200
Nope.

47
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:38,740
It is the same identifier every time that person signs into your specific service,

48
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:44,160
but it is completely different from the token the health ministry receives for that same person.

49
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:47,760
So no single ministry ever holds the central national number.

50
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:50,260
Okay, let's look at a practical example then.

51
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:53,460
We frequently look at the fictional country of Progresa.

52
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:59,360
Their identity authority has an active service, but, um, is it actually using this published block?

53
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:03,040
Well, no. Progresa is not using the published block.

54
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:07,500
They currently offer just one service, which is a legacy contract of their own design.

55
00:04:08,080 --> 00:04:11,760
Ah, so they're completely bypassing that bouncer and wristband consent model.

56
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:19,200
Exactly. Their examination authority performs a direct server-to-server read keyed exactly on the citizen's national number.

57
00:04:19,839 --> 00:04:23,440
Wow. So the citizen is not present to approve the sharing of their data at all.

58
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:28,040
No, they aren't. And while that direct read functions for their exams today,

59
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:30,600
it leaves them locked into one supplier.

60
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:38,780
Their architecture remains highly siloed, duplicated, and completely misses the published block's user-centric OpenID standard.

61
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:43,200
So I mean, the key takeaway for anyone running a ministry program is pretty clear.

62
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:50,500
The published identity block verifies the person and releases only what they, you know, actually approve through those specific tokens.

63
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:52,980
It issues no functional identity.

64
00:04:53,340 --> 00:05:00,620
Yeah, absolutely. You must verify what your authority actually runs today before you build a service on it, just

65
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:02,960
to avoid paying for duplicated effort.

66
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:03,520
Right.

